WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.288
Hi, I'm Kelp!

00:00:02.288 --> 00:00:05.947
Jing turns key presses and prompts into first-person video, with sound.

00:00:05.947 --> 00:00:08.276
Here's what those keys are wired to.

00:00:09.433 --> 00:00:13.551
The idea: six numbers reach all fifty layers, through injectors that start

00:00:13.551 --> 00:00:16.640
at zero, so control trains into a finished model.

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:21.408
Keys fill a six-slot camera vector.

00:00:21.408 --> 00:00:25.623
Since a September fix, four are live: forward, strafe, pitch and yaw.

00:00:25.623 --> 00:00:27.379
Rise and roll stay zero.

00:00:27.799 --> 00:00:31.259
A shared encoder lifts six numbers to five hundred and twelve.

00:00:31.259 --> 00:00:35.348
Fifty injectors, one per layer, turn that into a scale and a shift.

00:00:35.768 --> 00:00:39.803
Each modulates its layer's hidden state, in one trunk that carries

00:00:39.803 --> 00:00:41.270
prompt, video and audio.

00:00:41.270 --> 00:00:43.105
Even dialogue arrives as text.

00:00:43.525 --> 00:00:47.888
Scale and shift start at zero, so each injector starts out doing

00:00:47.888 --> 00:00:49.343
nothing: the ControlNet trick.

00:00:49.343 --> 00:00:52.252
About three hundred million parameters, under one percent.

00:00:53.483 --> 00:00:55.118
The repository gives the clock.

00:00:55.538 --> 00:01:00.140
One control slice is seventeen frames: point seven oh eight seconds, at

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:01.674
twenty-four frames a second.

00:01:02.094 --> 00:01:03.290
Times the slices.

00:01:03.290 --> 00:01:07.676
The bakery example sums to twenty-one: three hundred and sixty-two frames.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:11.371
Attention runs over the whole clip, both ways.

00:01:11.371 --> 00:01:15.520
Frame one depends on the last, so the first frame arrives with the last.

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:19.117
A causal model, coming soon, could emit frames as it goes.

00:01:19.117 --> 00:01:22.005
Even then, a key press waits for the next slice.

00:01:23.183 --> 00:01:27.386
So behind six numbers sits a lot of model: fifty injectors, one

00:01:27.386 --> 00:01:29.838
per layer, ticking on a seventeen-frame clock.

00:01:31.067 --> 00:01:36.381
To recap: six numbers, fifty injectors that start at zero, and a seventeen-frame clock.

00:01:36.801 --> 00:01:38.843
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:38.843 --> 00:01:39.426
I'm Kelp.

00:01:39.426 --> 00:01:39.718
Bye!
